.TH GGE\-ENGINE 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-engine \- the main loop, EngineConfig, Engine, timing, and main.c
.SH DESCRIPTION
The core module (engine.h) owns the window, the renderer, the input snapshot and the scene stack, and runs the loop that drives everything else. A game touches it in three places: \fBmain()\fR configures and starts it, scenes receive an \fBEngine *\fR in every callback, and scene code calls \fBengine_push\fR/\fBengine_pop\fR/\fBengine_replace\fR to change what is running.
.PP
.SH MAIN
Every game has exactly one \fBmain()\fR, and it is always the same shape:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include "engine.h"
#include "engine_main.h"   /* WinMain shim for Windows; include in this file only */
#include "scenes.h"
#include "game_state.h"
\&
static GameState state;    /* the whole saveable world, see gge\-state(7) */
\&
int main(void) {
  static Engine e;         /* static: it must outlive every scene */
  EngineConfig cfg = {
    .title = "Cats", .logical_w = 320, .logical_h = 180, .window_scale = 4,
    .user = &state, .org = "mystudio", .app = "cats",
    .audio = true, .text = true, .gamepad = true,
    .pause_scene = pause_scene(),
  };
  if (!engine_init(&e, &cfg)) return 1;
  game_state_init(&state);
  engine_push(&e, title_scene());
  engine_run(&e);
  engine_shutdown(&e);
  return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBengine_init\fR opens the window and starts the services the config asks for; \fBengine_push\fR queues the first scene; \fBengine_run\fR does not return until the game ends; \fBengine_shutdown\fR pops what is left and closes SDL. Projects made with \fBgge init\fR fill the config from \fBgge.conf\fR through generated GGE_* macros (gge\-build(7)); the shape is the same.
.PP
.SH ENGINECONFIG
Zero fields take defaults, so name only what you need. The full field list with defaults is in EngineConfig(3). The ones that shape a game:
.PP
.TP
\fBlogical_w, logical_h\fR
The resolution your code draws in. Everything (positions, mouse, text sizes) is in these units; the window scales the frame to fit and letterboxes the rest. Pick the size of your art: 320x180 or 640x360 for pixel art at 16:9, 256x224 for a retro 4:3 look. Changing it later means re\-laying\-out every UI position, so decide early.
.TP
\fBwindow_scale\fR
The initial window is logical x scale. The user can resize freely and F11 toggles borderless fullscreen; the logical size never changes.
.TP
\fBtick_rate\fR
Fixed simulation rate; 60 is right for nearly everything. Raise it only for fast physics; lower it (30, 20) for turn\-based or simulation games where per\-tick cost is high. \fBdt\fR in update is always exactly 1/tick_rate.
.TP
\fBuser\fR
Your GameState. The engine stores the pointer and never dereferences it.
.TP
\fBorg, app\fR
Name the per\-user save folder. Without both, saves go to the current directory, which is fine during development.
.TP
\fBaudio, text, gamepad\fR
Each opens a service; leaving one off saves startup time and avoids a log line on machines without the device. \fBtext\fR only matters when the engine was built with ENGINE_WITH_TTF.
.TP
\fBpause_scene\fR
A scene the engine pushes when the window loses focus. Give it your pause overlay so alt\-tabbing never lets the player get killed off\-screen. The engine checks that the scene is not already on top before pushing.
.TP
\fBclear_color\fR
What the frame is cleared to before any scene draws.
.SH THE LOOP, IN DETAIL
\fBengine_run\fR repeats this until \fBrunning\fR is false:
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTime.\fR The nanoseconds since the previous frame are added to an accumulator, capped at 8 ticks' worth so a stall (window drag, breakpoint) does not trigger a burst of hundreds of updates.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBInput.\fR \fBpoll_input\fR copies last frame's key, button and pad states into the \fBprev_*\fR arrays, drains SDL's event queue (quit, resize, focus, wheel, typed text, gamepad hot\-plug), then samples the keyboard, mouse and gamepad into \fBEngine.input\fR. F11 toggles fullscreen and F3 the debug overlay here, before scenes see anything. Focus loss pushes \fBpause_scene\fR if one is configured.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAudio.\fR The music stream is topped up so it loops.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhandle_input.\fR Called on the top scene only, with \fB&e\->input\fR. Once per displayed frame.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBapply_pending.\fR Pushes, pops and replaces queued so far take effect (on_exit / on_enter run here).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate.\fR While the accumulator holds at least one tick: walk the stack from the top down, calling \fBupdate(e, s, e\->dt)\fR on each scene until one has \fBblocks_update_below\fR set; then apply pending changes; subtract one tick. Zero ticks run on a frame that came too soon, two or more after a slow frame. This is why simulation must live in update: it advances the same amount of game time per tick no matter how the display behaves.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrender.\fR \fBalpha\fR = accumulator / tick length, in 0..1: how far the simulation is between the last tick and the next. The frame is cleared to \fBclear_color\fR, then scenes render from the topmost one with \fBblocks_render_below\fR set up to the top of the stack, each painting over the previous.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDebug overlay.\fR If F3 is on, a status line (fps, stack depth, top scene, pad) and every queued \fBdebug_rect\fR/\fBdebug_line\fR/\fBdebug_text\fR are drawn; the queues are cleared either way.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPresent.\fR The frame is shown (vsync), \fBframe\fR increments, and if the stack is empty the loop ends.
.SS Why fixed ticks
With a fixed \fBdt\fR the game plays identically on every machine, save files replay deterministically from a seed, and physics never explodes because one frame took too long. The cost is that a frame can land between ticks; \fBalpha\fR fixes that for rendering (gge\-drawing(7)), and nothing else needs to care.
.PP
.SS What runs at which rate
.IP \(bu 2
Display rate (once per frame): \fBhandle_input\fR, \fBrender\fR, everything inside them.
.IP \(bu 2
Tick rate (0..8 times per frame): \fBupdate\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Consequences: never move objects in \fBhandle_input\fR (they would move faster on faster monitors); never roll random numbers or play sounds in \fBrender\fR (they would repeat every frame); read keys for movement inside \fBupdate\fR through \fB&e\->input\fR, since \fBhandle_input\fR may not run on a given tick.
.SH ENGINE FIELDS A SCENE USES
The struct is described field by field in Engine(3). Day to day:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
GameState *g = e\->user;               /* your world */
float w = (float)e\->logical_w;        /* screen size for layout */
if (key_pressed(&e\->input, KEY_P)) ;  /* input from inside update */
save_write(&e\->save, "slot1", g, sizeof *g, SAVE_VERSION);
if (e\->frame % 30 == 0) ;             /* twice a second at 60 fps */
debug_text(4, 20, "fps %.0f", e\->fps);
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBwindow_w\fR/\fBwindow_h\fR are the real window size and are almost never needed; \fBdt\fR is the tick length if you need it outside update; \fBhas_audio\fR/\fBhas_text\fR tell you whether those services came up.
.PP
.SH CHANGING SCENES
\fBengine_push\fR, \fBengine_pop\fR and \fBengine_replace\fR never act immediately. They append to a queue that is applied after the current callback returns, which makes them safe to call from anywhere, including from inside a menu action or from the scene being popped. See gge\-scenes(7) for the stack semantics and engine_push(3), engine_pop(3), engine_replace(3), engine_top(3) for each call.
.PP
.SH ENDING THE GAME
Three ways, all clean: \fBengine_quit(e)\fR from anywhere; popping the last scene; the window's close button (which sets \fBinput.quit_requested\fR and ends the loop). In every case \fBengine_run\fR returns and \fBmain()\fR calls \fBengine_shutdown\fR, which runs \fBon_exit\fR for any scene still on the stack, top first, so sounds and music are released there as usual.
.PP
.SH WINDOW AND KEYS THE ENGINE OWNS
.TP
\fBF11\fR
Toggle borderless fullscreen (engine_toggle_fullscreen(3)). The logical size is unchanged; the picture is letterboxed.
.TP
\fBF3\fR
Toggle the debug overlay (gge\-debug(7)).
.TP
\fBResize\fR
The window is resizable; the logical frame scales to fit with letterboxing. Mouse coordinates in Input are already converted to logical pixels.
.TP
\fBFocus loss\fR
\fBpause_scene\fR is pushed; the loop sleeps 16 ms per frame while unfocused so a backgrounded game does not burn a core.
.SH TIMING RECIPES
.TP
\fBCooldowns and delays\fR
Use Timer (gge\-tween(7)) or subtract dt from a float in GameState.
.TP
\fBOnce per N ticks\fR
Keep a tick counter in GameState: \fBif (++g\->ticks % 30 == 0)\fR.
.TP
\fBReal\-time clock (speedrun timer)\fR
\fBg\->elapsed += dt\fR in update; format it in render.
.TP
\fBSlow motion / hit\-stop\fR
Scale dt yourself: \fBfloat sdt = dt * g\->time_scale;\fR and pass \fBsdt\fR to your entity ticks; keep UI on the real dt.
.TP
\fBPausing\fR
Do not stop the loop; push a scene with \fBblocks_update_below\fR (gge\-scenes(7)).
